A||||11||fierosound||Alternate throttle body for V6||||10||
Z||000000||fierosound||02-20-2000||04:07 PM||||Does anyone know what is available?  I think I remember seeing a message on a Camaro V6 site where he used a throttle body from a 3.1 on a 2.8.  Would a throttle body from a 3.1 or 3.4 fit on a Fiero's intake, or is the Fiero's throttle body "unique"?<p>[This message has been edited by fierosound (edited 02-20-2000).]||||reg||10
Z||000001||David Gamache||02-20-2000||05:21 PM||||I have never tried to swap throttle bodies, but the other gm units do look different on linkage etc... and i seen quite a few although anything may be possible. The fiero store sells a bored over throttle body you might want to check out.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Phil||02-20-2000||07:47 PM||||Another source is RS Motor Sports <A HREF="http://webhome.idirect.com/~rsm1/" TARGET=_blank>http://webhome.idirect.com/~rsm1/</A>  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||88formula||02-21-2000||08:28 PM||||The Fiero Store now sells bigger throttle bodies for the v6, check it out. <A HREF="http://www.secureleadercom.com/fierostore/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.secureleadercom.com/fierostore/</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||terryk||02-21-2000||08:39 PM||||Before you spend your bucks, keep in mind that much of the "improvement" you get is that you have more throttle opening at a lower throttle position. "Wow!, it pulls better even at part throttle!". <P>Unless you've done something significant to increase the VE of the engine, it is not a cost effective change.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Cooter||02-21-2000||08:53 PM||||Also keep in mind that big bore throttle bodies will drive an automatic transmission crazy. The engine will be getting more air, but at a lower throttle position. This will cause the tranny to shift early because the TV cable controls some of the upshift points and because . . . anyway, you get the point.||||reg||1
Z||000006||fierosound||02-27-2000||05:41 AM||||I wouldn't be changing the throttle body until I install a new 3.4L HO engine.  For those that have swapped their engine for a 3.4, how's the performance?  Wouldn't a larger throttle body be an improvement in this case?<BR>||||reg||10
Z||000007||lowCG||02-27-2000||10:49 AM||||The intake runner volume,and headwork would be first I'd think.You could make a progressive throttle linkeage that opens faster from half to full throttle,some cars do that to make them feel faster.<BR>I found a throttle body that I want;it's attached to a 3.4L,4cam motor.||||reg||1
Z||000008||DaRkLoRD||02-27-2000||04:31 PM||||"<I>You could make a progressive throttle linkeage that opens faster from half to full throttle,some cars do that to make them feel faster.</I>"<P>can you explain this? sounds interesting!<p>[This message has been edited by DaRkLoRD (edited 02-27-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||Patrick's Dad||02-27-2000||06:28 PM||||Hi Guys; new to the forum....<P>My TB froze on me. Had to get a used one from Kick Hill. Before that, I called a local boneyard, who said that they had one for me. It came out of a Beretta GT (2.8 V6), and was completely different. I since found out that Holley built the Fiero's TB, because GM didn't want to tool up for such a small run of parts. So, although any half decent tinkerer could make it work, they are different (The intake runners are different, too.). My inlaws have a Lumina with the 3.1, and I'd bet it's different, too.<P>This is something that I've tried to give much thought, as I'm working on a way to stuff more air into a 3.4 without a turbo/super charger....<P>||||reg||1
Z||000010||lowCG||02-27-2000||09:17 PM||||I had a fuel injected Opel way back,and it had a linkeage that had a cam with a little wheel that rode on it,was slow at first,then it opened all the way with only a slight shove.Changing a pivot point could do this in another way.<BR>I was in a GM junkyard a few years ago,when there was alot of 2.8s or 60DEG,V6s of different origin around,and they all had different intakes,so I wonder what the best one is?||||reg||1
Z||000011||fierosound||02-28-2000||03:16 AM||||In reviews of the Fiero I have read, "Pontiac did an excellent job designing their intake system".  Also, unlike Chevs, which use MAF (Mass Air Flow) sensors, Pontiac use MAP (Manifold Air Pressure) which is supposedly a better system for matching fuel/air mix.  ||||reg||10
